NEGATIVE CONTROL — captured result (git 2.34.1, Linux, 2026-07-14).

This is the EXPECTED outcome of the P6 negative control, NOT an inconclusive
unexecuted run. P6 attempts the pointer hijack via a *linked* fake gitdir (one
carrying a `commondir`). Git reads a linked worktree's config from the COMMON
dir, never from a plain `config` in the per-worktree gitdir, so the planted
`core.hooksPath`/`filter.*.clean` are IGNORED — git never reads them. The clean
"no host execution" below is therefore the control WORKING: it proves the
linked-gitdir path is a vector git ALREADY DEFEATS, so that specific path is NOT
a viable escape. It is NOT evidence the seam is closed.

The real, observed-red reproduction is the sibling probe P6b
(p6b-real-vector-standalone-alternates.{sh,out}, executed on the host
2026-07-14), which uses a STANDALONE fake gitdir (no `commondir`) +
`objects/info/alternates` — git DOES read that config, `hooksPath IS honored`,
and the host executes the agent's hook. P6b is the reproduction; P6 is the
negative control. No reader should read P6's clean result as "the escape
doesn't work" — read it as "this particular repoint is the one git defeats."
See PRD D10 / AC-7 (docs/prds/agent-workspace-isolation.md).

EXPECTED / OBSERVED (this run):
  STEP A: no host execution — git ignored the linked-gitdir config.
  STEP B: still no execution — no honored hook OR filter exists to kill here
          (the FIX-A-kills-hook / filter-survives result is P6b STEP 2).
  STEP C: pointer overwrite REFUSED under the immutable-pointer emulation.

